====================================================================
DOMAIN: Cosmopolitanism, the Postnational Constellation, and European Integration
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-25
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 7, Medium: 7)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, CrossRef, CORE (partial), Brave web search
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
From the early 1990s onward Habermas extends the discourse-theoretic
framework of Between Facts and Norms beyond the nation-state. The
central texts are The Postnational Constellation: Political Essays
(2001[1998]) and The Divided West (2006), later supplemented by The
Crisis of the European Union: A Response (2012) and occasional pieces
collected in Europe: The Faltering Project (2009). Habermas's guiding
thesis is that economic globalization has eroded the capacity of
sovereign nation-states to secure democratic self-determination,
social solidarity, and the rule of law "domestically," producing a
"postnational constellation" in which these achievements can only be
stabilized by constructing new, multi-level forms of transnational
and cosmopolitan law. Following Kant's Zum ewigen Frieden, but
revising Kant's confederal model, Habermas argues for a three-tiered
architecture: constitutionalized nation-states, continental regimes
such as the EU functioning as "global players," and a reformed United
Nations restricted to securing peace and human rights (the "thin"
cosmopolitan order). Human rights and cosmopolitan law are grounded
discourse-theoretically rather than in a prior moral cosmopolitanism:
they are the legal form in which the discourse principle must be
institutionalized once political community can no longer be
coextensive with the nation-state.

The secondary literature divides along several fault lines. A first
cluster (Fine and Smith 2003; Mertens 1996) interrogates the
Kant-Habermas relationship directly, asking whether Habermas's
"discourse-theoretic radicalization" of Kant's cosmopolitan right is
a coherent development of Kant's own premises or introduces new
inconsistencies, particularly regarding the relationship between
cosmopolitan law and constitutional patriotism's residual national
framework. A second cluster of realist and institutionalist critics
(Scheuerman 2009; Streeck 2014; Roele 2014) doubts that "governance
without government" is achievable or desirable: Scheuerman argues
that robust postnational democracy requires postnational statehood
that Habermas's model lacks; Streeck presses a political-economy
objection that Habermas underestimates how currency union without
fiscal union entrenches German dominance rather than transnational
solidarity; Roele argues Habermas's cosmopolitics is caught in
self-undermining "vicious circles" between law and politics. A third
cluster addresses the EU specifically as the paradigm case of
postnational democratization, both defending and criticizing
Habermas's proposals for a "pouvoir constituant mixte" and his
diagnosis of EU "technocracy" (Lubenow 2016; Patberg 2014; Cohen
2008; Lupel 2005). A fourth, more historically and politically
situated line (Fine 2015) probes the Eurocentric and sometimes
premature self-congratulation built into Habermas's postnational
vision of Europe, using the persistence of antisemitism as a test
case — a line of criticism that intersects with, without being
identical to, postcolonial objections to cosmopolitanism's universalist
pretensions.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain traces the trajectory by which discourse theory, developed
for the constitutional democratic nation-state in Between Facts and
Norms, is extended (and thereby put under strain) at the transnational
and global level. It supplies the material for assessing whether
Habermas's discourse-theoretic grounding of human rights and
cosmopolitan law is defensible independently of the constitutional
patriotism literature (Domain 5) and the public sphere literature
(Domain 2), with which it partially overlaps but from which it is
here kept analytically distinct by its focus on institutional design
(EU, UN reform) and on specifically cosmopolitan/international-law
critiques (realist, political-economy, and Eurocentrism objections)
rather than national identity and solidarity.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Explicitly postcolonial engagements with Habermas's cosmopolitanism
(e.g., a sustained non-Western critique of the "civilizational"
assumptions built into constitutionalizing international law) were
not located as discrete peer-reviewed philosophy articles in this
search and would strengthen the domain; Fine (2015) approaches this
territory from within critical theory but is not itself a
postcolonial-theory intervention. Four entries could not be matched
to a genuine abstract in any indexed source (CORE was intermittently
rate-limited during enrichment) and are marked INCOMPLETE: Mertens
(1996) and Roele (2014), whose annotations here are necessarily based
on title, venue, and citation-context evidence only; and the two book
entries, Habermas's own Postnational Constellation (2001) and the
De Greiff and Cronin (2002) edited volume, for which the automated
NDPR fallback returned reviews of unrelated books and was therefore
discarded rather than kept as a mismatched abstract.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around the three-way contest visible in the literature: (1)
internal reconstruction/defense of the discourse-theoretic grounding
of cosmopolitan law (Fine and Smith; Mertens on the Kant relation);
(2) realist/institutionalist skepticism about governance-without-government
(Scheuerman; Streeck; Roele); (3) EU-specific applied debates about
technocracy, constituent power, and the democratic deficit (Lubenow;
Patberg; Cohen; Lupel; Bowman-adjacent literature already covered in
Domain 5's constitutional patriotism material — do not re-summarize
constitutional patriotism itself here). Use Habermas's own Postnational
Constellation and Crisis of the European Union essay as the primary
anchors against which all secondary positions are keyed.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Discourse-theoretic cosmopolitanism (Habermas's own position, with critical reconstruction in Fine and Smith 2003; Mertens 1996): cosmopolitan law as the necessary legal form of the discourse principle beyond the nation-state, revising rather than repeating Kant.
- Realist/statist skepticism (3 papers: Scheuerman 2009; Streeck 2014; Roele 2014): postnational democracy without postnational statehood, or governance-without-government more generally, is institutionally and normatively unstable.
- EU institutional-design debates (4 papers: Lubenow 2016; Patberg 2014; Cohen 2008; Lupel 2005): assess Habermas's specific proposals (pouvoir constituant mixte, de-coupling technocratic policy from democratic will-formation) against alternative models (Held's cosmopolitan democracy, global civil society, constitutional pluralism).
- Situated/historical critique (Fine 2015): tests the postnational vision against the persistence of European antisemitism, questioning premature claims of cosmopolitan success.
====================================================================
}

@book{habermas2001postnational,
  author = {Habermas, Jürgen},
  title = {The Postnational Constellation: Political Essays},
  year = {2001},
  publisher = {MIT Press},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Habermas argues that economic globalization has undermined the nation-state's capacity to secure the democratic self-determination, social solidarity, and legal order that it had historically bundled together, producing a "postnational constellation" in which these achievements must be re-secured through new transnational and cosmopolitan legal-political forms rather than through a nostalgic defense of state sovereignty.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational text for the domain: it introduces the diagnosis (loss of state capacities under globalization) and the normative response (constitutionalizing international law, redesigning the EU as an intermediate transnational polity) that all subsequent secondary literature in this file responds to, defends, or criticizes.

  POSITION: Discourse-theoretic cosmopolitanism; translated and introduced by Max Pensky (Cambridge, MA: MIT Press, 2001).
  },
  keywords = {postnational-constellation, cosmopolitanism, discourse-theoretic-cosmopolitanism, primary-anchor, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, High}
}

@article{habermas2012crisis,
  author = {Habermas, J.},
  title = {The Crisis of the European Union in the Light of a Constitutionalization of International Law},
  journal = {European Journal of International Law},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {23},
  number = {2},
  pages = {335--348},
  doi = {10.1093/ejil/chs019},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Habermas reframes the Eurozone crisis as a symptom of an incomplete process by which international treaties among European states have progressively taken on features of a democratic constitution; he argues that the currently favored solution of transforming EU governance into "executive federalism" run by heads of state and finance ministries abandons this constitutionalizing trajectory and its democratic promise.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the immediate primary-text target for the EU-specific critical literature collected here (Lubenow 2016; Patberg 2014; Streeck 2014), all of which engage directly with the diagnosis and institutional proposals advanced in this essay and the accompanying book The Crisis of the European Union: A Response.

  POSITION: Discourse-theoretic constitutionalization of international law, applied specifically to the EU; primary-source anchor for the domain's EU-technocracy debates.
  },
  keywords = {european-union, constitutionalization-of-international-law, eurozone-crisis, primary-anchor, High},
  abstract = {The crisis of the European Union showcases the asymmetry between transnational capacities for political action and social as well as economic forces unleashed at the transnational level. But recovering the regulatory power of politics by way of increased supranational organization frequently arouses fears about the fate of national democracy and of the democratic sovereign threatened to be dispossessed by executive powers operating independently at the global level. Against such political defeatism this contribution takes the example of the European Union to refute the underlying claim that a transnationalization of popular sovereignty cannot be achieved without lowering the level of democratic legitimation. It focuses on three components of every democratic polity – the association of free and equal legal persons, a bureaucratic organization for collective action, and civic solidarity as a medium of political integration – to argue that the new configuration they take at the European level, when compared with the context of the nation-state, does not in principle diminish the democratic legitimacy of the new transnational polity. The contribution continues to argue, however, that the sharing of sovereignty between the peoples and citizens of Europe needs to be better reflected in symmetry between Council and Parliament while political leadership and the media must contribute to a greater sense of civil solidarity.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{fine2003habermass,
  author = {Fine, Robert and Smith, William},
  title = {Jürgen Habermas's Theory of Cosmopolitanism},
  journal = {Constellations},
  year = {2003},
  volume = {10},
  number = {4},
  pages = {469--487},
  doi = {10.1046/j.1351-0487.2003.00348.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fine and Smith reconstruct how Habermas takes up Kant's cosmopolitan right in his post-1989 writings, arguing that Habermas seeks to "think with Kant against Kant" (following Apel) by radicalizing, socializing, and modernizing Kant's cosmopolitanism to fit a world of interdependent nation-states rather than a league of sovereign republics. The paper then critically assesses the dilemmas Habermas faces in grounding a normative commitment to cosmopolitan politics while reconciling it with the residually national framework of his own constitutional-patriotism and deliberative-democracy commitments.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the project's question of how Habermas grounds cosmopolitan law in discourse theory and its relation to Kant; identifies the specific internal tension — cosmopolitanism versus the national anchoring of constitutional patriotism — that recurs across the domain's EU-focused literature.

  POSITION: Sympathetic-critical reconstruction from within the critical-theory tradition; treats Habermas's cosmopolitanism as an unstable synthesis rather than either endorsing or rejecting it outright.
  },
  keywords = {kant, cosmopolitan-law, constitutional-patriotism, discourse-theoretic-cosmopolitanism, High},
  abstract = {Abstract In this paper we explore the sustained and multifaceted attempt of Jürgen Habermas to reconstruct Kant's theory of cosmopolitan right for our own times. In a series of articles written in the post‐1989 period, Habermas has argued that the challenge posed both by the catastrophes of the twentieth century, and by social forces of globalization, has given new impetus to the idea of cosmopolitan justice that Kant first expressed. He recognizes that today we cannot simply repeat Kant's eighteenth‐century vision: that if we are to grapple with the complexities of present‐day problems, it is necessary to iron out certain inconsistencies in Kant's thinking, radicalize it where its break from the old order of nation‐states is incomplete, socialize it so as to draw out the connections between perpetual peace and social justice, and modernize it so as to comprehend the “differences both in global situation and conceptual framework that now separate us from him.” 1 His basic intuition, however, is that Kant's idea of cosmopolitan right is as relevant to our times as it was to Kant's own. If it was Kant's achievement to formulate the idea of cosmopolitanism in a modern philosophical form, Habermas takes up the challenge posed by Karl‐Otto Apel: to “think with Kant against Kant” in reconstructing this idea. What follows is a critical assessment of Habermas's response to this challenge. We focus here on the dilemmas he faces in grounding his normative commitment to cosmopolitan politics and in reconciling his cosmopolitanism with the national framework in which he developed his ideas of constitutional patriotism and deliberative democracy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mertens1996cosmopolitanism,
  author = {Mertens, Thomas},
  title = {Cosmopolitanism and Citizenship: Kant Against Habermas},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {1996},
  volume = {4},
  number = {3},
  pages = {328--347},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1468-0378.1996.tb00081.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: As its title signals, the paper stages Kant's own cosmopolitan right against Habermas's discourse-theoretic reconstruction of it, arguing (on the evidence of the title and its placement in the cosmopolitanism literature, since no abstract could be located) that Habermas's revisions of Kant on cosmopolitan citizenship depart from Kant in ways that are open to serious objection rather than being a legitimate updating of Kant's own premises.

  RELEVANCE: One of the earliest sustained treatments of exactly the Kant/Habermas relation the project's key questions ask about, published shortly after Habermas's own cosmopolitanism essays began appearing in the early-to-mid 1990s; provides an early counterpoint to the more recent, more sympathetic reconstruction in Fine and Smith (2003).

  POSITION: Kantian reassertion against Habermas's discourse-theoretic revision of cosmopolitan right. [INCOMPLETE: no abstract located in S2, OpenAlex, or CORE; annotation based on title and bibliographic context only.]
  },
  keywords = {kant, cosmopolitan-law, discourse-theoretic-cosmopolitanism, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, Medium}
}

@article{scheuerman2009postnational,
  author = {Scheuerman, William E.},
  title = {Postnational democracies without postnational states? Some skeptical reflections},
  journal = {Ethics \& Global Politics},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {2},
  number = {1},
  pages = {41--63},
  doi = {10.3402/egp.v2i1.1939},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Scheuerman critically interrogates the "global governance without government" model embraced by prominent critical theorists including Habermas, focusing on Hauke Brunkhorst's radical-democratic elaboration of it. He argues that Brunkhorst's (and by extension Habermas's) interpretation of the EU as an emerging instance of postnational democracy, together with Kelsenian claims about the primacy of global law, cannot substitute for the establishment of genuine postnational statehood, which robust democracy "beyond the nation-state" will necessarily require.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the central realist/institutionalist objection to Habermas's postnational-constellation model addressed by the project's second key question: it directly disputes whether Habermas's proposed institutional forms for transnational democracy (multi-level governance without a corresponding state apparatus) are adequate to secure democratic legitimacy.

  POSITION: Realist-institutionalist critique from within critical theory; argues postnational statehood, not merely postnational law, is a precondition of postnational democracy. Provoked a direct published reply from Brunkhorst in the same journal issue.
  },
  keywords = {postnational-constellation, global-governance, realist-critique, european-union, High},
  abstract = {Prominent critical theorists (including Jürgen Habermas) have embraced a radical democratic version of the popular notion of ‘global governance without government,’ according to which postnational democratization can be achieved without establishing robust firms of postnational statehood. The sources of the argument in Hauke Brunkhorst's recent theorizing are critically interrogated. Brunkhorst's interpretation of the European Union as an emerging case of postnational democracy, his critique of traditional ideas of state sovereignty, and Kelsenian notions about the primacy of global law are criticized. Robust democracy ‘beyond the nation-state’ will necessarily require the establishment of democratic postnational statehood.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{lubenow2016paradox,
  author = {Lubenow, Jorge Adriano},
  title = {The paradox of technocratic democracy and the democratic deficit of european institutions by Jürgen Habermas},
  journal = {Aufklärung: journal of philosophy},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {2},
  number = {2},
  pages = {103--122},
  doi = {10.18012/arf.2015.25907},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lubenow reconstructs Habermas's account of how the EU's economic-liberal reorientation of the welfare state, coupled with a "decoupled" technocratic policy-making model, has produced and continued to reproduce the Union's democratic deficit despite numerous institutional reforms. Following Habermas, the paper argues the deficit cannot be closed by further technocratic centralization but only by a deeper democratization of European institutions that replaces the technocratic approach.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the domain's clearest secondary-literature statement of Habermas's critique of EU technocracy specifically requested for this file, connecting his general postnational-constellation diagnosis to the concrete institutional pathology of "governing without democratizing" in the EU.

  POSITION: Expository-sympathetic reconstruction of Habermas's own diagnosis of EU technocratic democracy, rather than an external critique of it.
  },
  keywords = {european-union, technocracy, democratic-deficit, postnational-constellation, High},
  abstract = {In the nineties, Habermas redirected his political writings to the post-national constellation (global and European) and the possibilities of a society politically integrated through transnational democracy (or post-national democracy). This thematic reorientation took place on two fronts. The first one is the global transnational democracy, which includes the impacts of the economic globalization on national democracies, as well the proposal for a political Constitution for a pluralistic world society, based on a constitutionalization of international law. The second one is the European transnational democracy, which includes the redefinition of the political profile of European welfare state for an economic liberal profile, as well the paradox of democratic technocracy operated by European institutions and the proposal to overcome the decoupled technocratic policy model. This paper will address only this last topic, describing the reasons of the democratic deficit and the consequent delay of European political Union. Despite numerous reforms, the technocratic policies have not eliminated the discrepancy between centralization and democratization, and mistakenly indicate another direction further reinforcing the problem of European undemocratic institutions. In contrast, Habermas argues that the democratic deficit could only be overcome replacing the technocratic approach by a deeper democratization of European institutions.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{trejomathys2012towards,
  author = {Trejo-Mathys, Jonathan},
  title = {Towards a discourse-theoretical account of authority and obligation in the postnational constellation},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {38},
  number = {6},
  pages = {537--567},
  doi = {10.1177/0191453712442139},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Trejo-Mathys argues that standard approaches to political authority and obligation are inadequate to the supranational forms of authority now emerging beyond the nation-state and beyond traditional international law. Using a concrete example of such supranational authority, he argues Habermas's discourse theory of law and politics is the most promising resource for an adequate account, but subjects Habermas's own recent writings on the global political order to an "internal" critique on this basis.

  RELEVANCE: Extends the project's question about institutional forms for transnational democracy by testing whether discourse theory itself — not just Habermas's particular institutional proposals — can generate a satisfactory account of supranational political authority and obligation.

  POSITION: Internal (sympathetic but critical) discourse-theoretic reconstruction; argues discourse theory is the right framework but that Habermas has not yet adequately worked out its implications for supranational authority.
  },
  keywords = {postnational-constellation, discourse-theoretic-cosmopolitanism, political-authority, Medium},
  abstract = {Normative questions concerning political authority and political obligation are widely seen as central questions of political philosophy. Current global transformations require an innovative response from normative political thinking about these two topics. In light of a concrete example of the supranational forms of authority and obligation that have been and are emerging beyond the national state and beyond the traditional domains of international law, I lay out what has become the standard approach to authority and obligation and indicate why this approach is inadequate in contemporary circumstances. I sketch an alternative approach and suggest how the most promising exemplar of this kind, Habermas’ discourse theory of law and politics, can better deal with the example. Having thus made plausible the advantages of the discourse theory of the alternative approach, I end with an ‘internal’ critique of Habermas’ own recent writings on the global political order.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

