  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This edited collection, assembled by two of Habermas's principal English translators, gathers essays on the moral and political challenges globalization poses to justice and democratic legitimacy, situating Habermas's own postnational-constellation writings within a wider field of contemporaneous work on transnational politics and global justice rather than treating them as a self-standing intervention.

  RELEVANCE: Named specifically in this domain's research brief as a key collection bridging Habermas's own cosmopolitan/postnational writings and their critical reception; functions as a hinge text between the primary Habermas anchors in this file and the secondary critical literature (Fine and Smith 2003; Cohen 2008) responding to the same debates.

  POSITION: Edited volume spanning multiple positions on global justice and transnational politics rather than advancing a single thesis; reviewed in Ethics \& International Affairs 16 (2002): 159-161.
  },
